+	<TEXT>
+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_LEADER_GRACE_OMALLEY_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[H1]Grace O'Malley[\H1][NEWLINE][BOLD]Head of the � M�ille dynasty[\BOLD][NEWLINE][BOLD]Lived:[\BOLD] c. 1530 - c. 1603[NEWLINE][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Background:[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Grace O'Malley (Irish: Gr�inne N� Mh�ille) was the head of the � M�ille dynasty in the west of Ireland, and the daughter of E�ghan Dubhdara � M�ille.[PARAGRAPH:1]Upon her father's death, she took over active leadership of the lordship by land and sea, despite having a brother, D�nal an Ph�opa � M�ille. Marriage to D�nal an Chogaidh (Donal "of the war") � Flaithbheartaigh brought her greater wealth and influence, reportedly owning as much as 1,000 head of cattle and horses. In 1593, when her sons Tibbot Bourke and Murchadh � Flaithbheartaigh (Murrough O'Flaherty) and her half-brother D�nal an Ph�opa ("Donal of the Pipes") were taken captive by the English governor of Connacht, Sir Richard Bingham, O'Malley sailed to England to petition for their release. She formally presented her request to Queen Elizabeth I at her court in Greenwich Palace.[PARAGRAPH:1]O'Malley is not mentioned in the Irish annals, so documentary evidence for her life comes mostly from English sources, especially the eighteen "Articles of Interrogatory", questions put to her in writing on behalf of Elizabeth I. She is mentioned in the English State Papers and in other documents of the kind.[PARAGRAPH:1]In Irish folklore she is commonly known as Gr�inne Mhaol (anglicised as Granuaile) and is a well-known historical figure in sixteenth-century Irish history. Her name was also rendered in contemporaneous English documents in various ways, including Gr�inne O'Maly, Graney O'Mally, Granny ni Maille, Grany O'Mally, Grayn Ny Mayle, Grane ne Male, Grainy O'Maly, and Granee O'Maillie, rarely as Grace O'Malley. In popular culture, she is often referred to as "The Pirate Queen".</English>
