+	<!-- custom: based on https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Collins_(Irish_leader) -->
+	<TEXT>
+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_LEADER_MICHAEL_COLLINS_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[H1]Michael Collins[\H1][NEWLINE][BOLD]Irish revolutionary, soldier and politician[\BOLD][NEWLINE][BOLD]Lived:[\BOLD] 16 January 1922 - 22 August 1922[NEWLINE][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Background:[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Michael Collins (Irish: M�che�l � Coile�in) was an Irish revolutionary, soldier and politician who was a leading figure in the early-20th century struggle for Irish independence. During the War of Independence he was Director of Intelligence of the Irish Republican Army (IRA). He served in the government of the self-declared Irish Republic as the Minister for Home Affairs and later as the Minister for Finance. He was Chairman of the Provisional Government of the Irish Free State from January 1922 and commander-in-chief of the National Army from July until his death in an ambush in August 1922, during the Civil War.[PARAGRAPH:1]Collins was born in Woodfield, County Cork, the youngest of eight children. He moved to London in 1906 to become a clerk in the Post Office Savings Bank at Blythe House. He was a member of the London GAA, through which he became associated with the Irish Republican Brotherhood and the Gaelic League. He returned to Ireland in January 1916 and fought in the Easter Rising. He was taken prisoner and held in the Frongoch internment camp as a prisoner of war, but he was released in December 1916.[PARAGRAPH:1]Collins subsequently rose through the ranks of the Irish Volunteers and Sinn F�in. He was elected as MP for South Cork in December 1918. Sinn F�in's elected members (later known as TDs) formed an Irish parliament, the First D�il, in January 1919 and declared the independence of the Irish Republic. Collins was appointed Minister for Finance. In the ensuing War of Independence, he was Director of Organisation and Adjutant General for the Irish Volunteers, and Director of Intelligence of the IRA. He gained fame as a guerrilla warfare strategist, planning many successful attacks on British forces together with 'the Squad', such as the "Bloody Sunday" assassinations of key British intelligence agents in November 1920.[PARAGRAPH:1]After the July 1921 ceasefire, Collins was one of five plenipotentiaries sent by the D�il cabinet at the request of �amon de Valera, to negotiate peace terms in London. The resulting Anglo-Irish Treaty, signed in December 1921, would establish the Irish Free State but depended on an oath of allegiance to the Crown. De Valera and other republican leaders found this clause of the treaty most difficult to accept. Collins viewed the treaty as offering "the freedom to achieve freedom", and helped persuade a majority of the D�il to ratify the treaty. A provisional government was formed under his chairmanship in early 1922. During this time he secretly provided support for an IRA offensive in Northern Ireland. It was soon disrupted by the Irish Civil War, in which Collins was commander-in-chief of the National Army. He was shot and killed in an ambush by anti-Treaty forces in August 1922.</English>
+	</TEXT>
